## Herdr dispatch and readiness contract

### Prefer the native agent seam

- For a recognized live agent, use `herdr agent prompt <agent-name-or-pane-id> "..."`.
  This is the atomic text-plus-Enter prompt seam and is the default dispatch and
  handoff mechanism.
- Give a live agent a stable target with `herdr agent rename <target> <name>`.
  `herdr pane rename` changes only a display label; it does not create a native
  agent identity.
- For a new ready shell whose readiness is reliable, use
  `herdr agent start <name> --kind <kind> --pane <id>`, then prompt the named
  agent through the native seam.
- For an ordinary shell or process, use `herdr pane run <pane-id> <command>...`.
  Raw `herdr pane send-text` followed by `herdr pane send-keys ... Enter` is
  only a clearly marked compatibility/raw-terminal fallback, never the default
  recognized-agent handoff.

### Startup recovery and trust

- If first-run bootstrap auto-updates and exits after a misleading ready signal,
  classify the target as `startup_failed`; do not prompt the stale target. Allow
  the update to finish and perform one bounded clean restart. If native startup
  remains unavailable, launch the CLI from a fresh shell with atomic
  `herdr pane run` and rely on proactive artifact handoff rather than coordinator
  polling.
- If Codex blocks before reading this brief and asks whether to trust the working
  directory, classify the target as `blocked`, never `working`. Verify the exact
  cwd, expected Git remote, branch, and task scope first. Approve trust only for
  the explicitly authorized workspace; otherwise stop and report the blocker.
  Never blindly answer an arbitrary approval prompt.

### Safe context reuse

- Before reusing a sub-panel CLI, determine its prior context and whether `/new`
  is safely supported. Reset only a safely completed context, then wait for fresh
  readiness before assigning work.
- If reset is not safe, close only a completed pane when it is safe to do so and
  create or start a fresh pane. Never assign work while an old turn may still be
  running. This preserves context/token isolation and at-most-once execution.

### Completed worker cleanup

- After atomically writing `report.md` or `review.md`, sending exactly one
  native completion handoff, and stopping work, the worker must not close,
  manipulate, or reuse its pane. A worker also must not open a subagent,
  background agent, watcher, second CLI, extra pane/tab, headless task, or
  self-dispatch path.
- The coordinator performs exactly one bounded liveness check. Only after it
  confirms that the old turn cannot still run or hold an uncommitted tool
  operation does the coordinator close the completed worker CLI/pane with
  `herdr pane close <pane-id>` (or the current equivalent) to release memory.
- Completed subtasks are closed by default, not retained as idle panes. Reuse
  is allowed only for an unfinished or safely reset context under a new
  coordinator dispatch. If handoff fails, record the failure and let the
  coordinator choose safe close/recovery; do not resend or leave a watcher.

